---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **한국 차트가 먼저였나.** (`/home-first`)
 *
 * ── 왜 이 지면인가 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 * 44편째 기사(`/article/korea-does-not-chart-it-first`)를 내면서 자료를 새로 만들었는데,
 * **그 자료를 볼 수 있는 곳이 어디에도 없었다.** 우리 공유 카드에는 이렇게 적혀 있다 —
 * 「every figure has a table behind it」. 표가 없으면 그 줄이 거짓말이 된다.
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 **말하지 못하는 것**을 지면 안에 박는다 ─────────
 * ⛔ 「같은 주」는 「같은 날」이 아니다. 넷플릭스는 주 단위로만 낸다 —
 *    엿새 이하의 앞섬은 **없는 것이 아니라 우리가 못 보는 것**이다.
 * ⛔ 앞을 보는 물음(다음에 뭐가 뜨나)은 팔지 않는다(/terms 와 같은 말이라야 한다).
 * ⛔ 순위표가 아니다. 세 가지 셈을 **나란히 놓고 왜 다른지**를 옆에 적는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 *
 * ── ⚠ 수를 여기 손으로 적지 않는다 ────────────────────────────
 * DESC 까지 자료에서 만든다. 8월 7일에 448편이 406편으로 바뀌었을 때
 * **주석과 DESC 에 박아 둔 수만 옛것으로 남았다.** 같은 자리를 두 번 밟지 않는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-home-first.json';

const TITLE = 'Did Korea chart it first?';

const 전부 = data.all;
const 좁은 = data.cleanest;
const 드라마 = data.byFormat[0];
const 영화 = data.byFormat[1];

const DESC =
  `For the ${data.titleCount} Korean titles that reached a Netflix Top 10 in Southeast Asia, `
  + `Korea's own chart got there first for ${전부.homeFirst}, in the same week for ${전부.sameWeek}, `
  + `and never charted ${data.neverAtHome} of them at all.`;

/** 네 갈래를 한 막대로. 「한국에 없음」을 빼면 그림이 실제보다 좋아 보인다 */
const 갈래 = [
  { 이름: 'Korea first', n: 전부.homeFirst, 색: 'a' },
  { 이름: 'Same week', n: 전부.sameWeek, 색: 'b' },
  { 이름: 'Korea later', n: 전부.awayFirst, 색: 'c' },
  { 이름: 'Never in Korea', n: data.neverAtHome, 색: 'd' },
];
const 갈래합 = 갈래.reduce((s, g) => s + g.n, 0);

const 셈들 = [
  { 이름: 'All titles that charted in both', g: 전부 },
  { 이름: "The panel's first week removed", g: data.censoredExcluded },
  { 이름: 'Shared names also removed', g: 좁은 },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  temporalCoverage: `${data.weekFrom}/${data.weekTo}`,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://query.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd} canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/home-first">
  <h1>Did Korea chart it first?</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    {data.titleCount} Korean titles have reached a Netflix Top 10 in {data.region} across
    {' '}{data.weekCount} weeks, {data.weekFrom} to {data.weekTo}. For each one we took the first
    week it charted anywhere in those six markets, and the first week it charted in {data.home}.
  </p>
  {/* 물음을 지면에 그대로 낸다. 자료 파일에 적어 둔 문장과 지면이 다른 물음을 말하면 안 된다 */}
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>Korea led for {전부.homeFirst} of {data.titleCount}</h2>
    <ul class="dist">
      {갈래.map((g) => (
        <li>
          <span class="k">{g.이름}</span>
          <span class={`bar s${g.색}`} style={`--w:${(g.n / 갈래합) * 100}%`}></span>
          <span class="v"><b>{g.n}</b> · {((100 * g.n) / 갈래합).toFixed(1)}%</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      The largest group by far is the one where the two charts move together. The second largest is
      the one where Korea's chart never carried the title at all — <b>{data.neverAtHome}</b> titles,
      {' '}<b>{data.neverAtHomePc}%</b> of everything that reached Southeast Asia.
      {' '}<a href="/article/korea-does-not-chart-it-first">The full story &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The same measurement, three ways</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Counted as</th><th>Titles</th><th>Korea first</th><th>Same week</th><th>Korea later</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {셈들.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{r.이름}</td>
              <td>{r.g.n}</td>
              <td>{r.g.homeFirst} <span class="pc">{r.g.homeFirstPc}%</span></td>
              <td class="tot">{r.g.sameWeek} <span class="pc">{r.g.sameWeekPc}%</span></td>
              <td>{r.g.awayFirst} <span class="pc">{r.g.awayFirstPc}%</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Why three rows and not one.</b> The panel begins at {data.weekFrom}, so a title already
      charting that week may have started earlier than we can see — {data.censoredCount} of them are
      removed in the second row. And a title is keyed by its name, so a film and a series sharing a
      name look like one work that led or lagged by years; the {data.sharedNameCount} names in that
      position come out in the third. <b>The same-week share moves by
      {' '}{(Math.max(...셈들.map((r) => r.g.sameWeekPc)) - Math.min(...셈들.map((r) => r.g.sameWeekPc))).toFixed(1)} points
      across all three.</b> We print all of them rather than pick the flattering one.
    </p>
    {/*
      ⛔ 뺀 것을 **이름으로** 낸다. 「9편을 뺐다」만 적으면 읽는 사람은 무엇이 빠졌는지 모른다.
         뺀 목록을 감추는 것이 수를 고르는 것보다 나쁘다.
    */}
    <p class="note">
      <b>The names we set aside.</b> {data.sharedNameTitles.join(', ')} — {data.sharedNameCount} titles
      where a Korean film and a Korean series share a name, {data.sharedNameInBoth} of them inside the
      group that charted on both sides. They stay in every count above, where only the first week
      matters, and come out of anything measured in weeks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>"Same week" is not "same day"</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      Netflix publishes one list per week. A title that entered Korea's chart on a Monday and
      Jakarta's on the following Friday appears here as a single simultaneous entry.{' '}
      <b>Any lead shorter than seven days is invisible to this table.</b> That is a limit of the
      source, not a finding, and it is the first thing to know before reading the row above.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>When one side led</th><th>Titles</th><th>Median lead</th><th>Longest</th><th>Led by one week</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Korea led</td>
            <td>{좁은.homeFirst}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.leadWeeks.homeFirstMedian} weeks</td>
            <td>{data.leadWeeks.homeFirstMax} weeks</td>
            <td>{data.leadWeeks.homeFirstOneWeek}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="nm">Southeast Asia led</td>
            <td>{좁은.awayFirst}</td>
            <td class="tot">{data.leadWeeks.awayFirstMedian} weeks</td>
            <td>{data.leadWeeks.awayFirstMax} weeks</td>
            <td>{data.leadWeeks.awayFirstOneWeek}</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The two sides are not mirror images. When Korea led, it led by a median of
      {' '}{data.leadWeeks.homeFirstMedian} weeks. When Southeast Asia led, the median was
      {' '}{data.leadWeeks.awayFirstMedian} weeks — <b>those are catalogue titles finding a second
      audience abroad a year or two later</b>, not releases running ahead of Korea. Measured in weeks
      here excludes the shared names, for the reason in the row above.
    </p>

    {/*
      ⛔ 순위표가 아니다. 두 끝을 **나란히** 놓고 무엇이 다른지를 아래에 적는다.
         수만 있는 표는 읽는 사람이 자기 눈으로 확인할 수가 없다.
    */}
    <h3>The longest gaps on each side</h3>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <h4>Korea led by</h4>
        <ul>
          {data.examples.homeFirst.map((e) => (
            <li><b>{e.title}</b> <span>{e.weeks} weeks &middot; {e.type}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Southeast Asia led by</h4>
        <ul>
          {data.examples.awayFirst.map((e) => (
            <li><b>{e.title}</b> <span>{e.weeks} weeks &middot; {e.type}</span></li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>The right-hand column is not the left-hand column reversed.</b> Its gaps are two to four
      years wide, which is not a release schedule — those are older titles that Southeast Asian
      audiences found on Netflix long before the same title surfaced on Korea's chart. The left-hand
      gaps are mostly films arriving on Netflix in Korea after a cinema run and reaching the rest of
      the region later still.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Series enter together. Films do not.</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Format</th><th>Titles</th><th>Korea first</th><th>Same week</th><th>Korea later</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.byFormat.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{f.format}</td>
              <td>{f.n}</td>
              <td>{f.homeFirst} <span class="pc">{f.homeFirstPc.toFixed(1)}%</span></td>
              <td class="tot">{f.sameWeek} <span class="pc">{f.sameWeekPc.toFixed(1)}%</span></td>
              <td>{f.awayFirst} <span class="pc">{f.awayFirstPc.toFixed(1)}%</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Part of this is mechanical and we are not hiding it.</b> A Netflix series is released to
      every country at once, so the only thing that can separate the two charts is how hard each one
      is to enter — {드라마.sameWeekPc}% of series enter both in the same week. Korean films often run
      in Korean cinemas long before reaching Netflix anywhere, and they split almost evenly between
      arriving in Korea first ({영화.homeFirst}) and arriving later ({영화.awayFirst}).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>{data.allSixNeverHome.length} titles charted in all six countries and never once in Korea</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Title</th><th>Format</th><th>First week in Southeast Asia</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.allSixNeverHome.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">{t.title}</td>
              <td class="nm">{t.type}</td>
              <td>{t.firstAway}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>This is not a room problem.</b> Korea's list has exactly the same number of places as
      Singapore's — <b>{data.slotsPerCountry.toLocaleString('en-US')} rows each</b>, ten titles times
      two lists times {data.weekCount} weeks. The collector refuses to run if those counts differ
      between any two of the seven markets, because the whole comparison rests on them being equal.
    </p>
  </section>

  {data.broadcasterTest && (
    <section>
      <h2>An explanation we tested and dropped</h2>
      <p class="note lead-in">
        Our first guess: these are television dramas Koreans watched when they aired on KBS, SBS, tvN
        or JTBC, so they never needed Netflix for them. If that were right, the never-charted group
        should be full of titles with a Korean broadcaster and the charted group should not.
      </p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Series</th><th>With a recorded Korean broadcaster</th><th>Share</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">Never charted in Korea</td>
              <td>{data.broadcasterTest.seriesNever.withBroadcaster} of {data.broadcasterTest.seriesNever.n}</td>
              <td class="tot">{data.broadcasterTest.seriesNever.pc}%</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="nm">Charted in Korea</td>
              <td>{data.broadcasterTest.seriesCharted.withBroadcaster} of {data.broadcasterTest.seriesCharted.n}</td>
              <td class="tot">{data.broadcasterTest.seriesCharted.pc}%</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        It goes the wrong way. The titles that <em>did</em> chart in Korea are the ones more likely to
        have aired on Korean television, and we do not have a replacement explanation.{' '}
        <b> One detail nearly fooled us.</b> Ignoring format, the same comparison reads
        {' '}{data.broadcasterTest.mixedNever.pc.toFixed(1)}% against {data.broadcasterTest.mixedCharted.pc}% —
        a gap three times larger, pointing the way we expected. That gap is format mix: the
        never-charted group is {data.neverAtHomeByFormat.films} films and
        {' '}{data.neverAtHomeByFormat.series} series, films rarely carry a broadcaster field at all,
        and the number was measuring how many films were in each group.
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/home-first" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li>
        <b>The groups add up.</b> {data.bothCharted} charted in both and {data.neverAtHome} never
        charted in {data.home}, which is {data.titleCount} — the full set of titles that charted in
        the six markets. It is the same {data.titleCount} that <a href="/titles">the title panel</a>
        {' '}and <a href="/reach">the country-reach page</a> read, and the collector refuses to run if
        that count disagrees with theirs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Not a forecast.</b> This says how often Korea's chart has been the earlier one so far. It
        cannot tell you whether the next Korean title will follow that pattern, and we do not sell
        the version that claims to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Chart entry, not release.</b> A title appearing here means it entered a top ten, not that
        it became available. Korean films reach Netflix after a cinema run that has no equivalent
        abroad, and none of that is visible in this data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Chart entry, not viewing.</b> Netflix publishes ten rows per country per week and no hours
        figure for country lists. Nothing on this page says how much anything was watched.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Six markets, not all of Southeast Asia.</b> Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar and Brunei publish no
        list and nothing here describes them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <p class="back"><a href="/">&larr; Back to the front page</a></p>
</WikiTip>

<style>
  h1 { margin: 0 0 .5rem; font-size: 1.7rem; letter-spacing: -.02em; }
  .lead { margin: 0 0 2rem; font-size: 1rem; color: var(--muted); }
  section { margin: 0 0 2.5rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 0 0 .7rem; letter-spacing: -.01em; }
  .note { font-size: .82rem; color: var(--muted); margin: .9rem 0 0; line-height: 1.6; }
  .note.lead-in { margin: 0 0 .9rem; }
  .note b { color: var(--ink); }
  .q { margin: -1.4rem 0 2rem; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--accent); }

  /* 두 끝을 나란히. 좁은 화면에서는 위아래로 접힌다 — 가로로 밀리면 안 된다 */
  h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 1.8rem 0 .2rem; }
  h4 { font-size: .88rem; margin: 0 0 .4rem; color: var(--accent); }
  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.2rem; margin-top: .6rem; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .two ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .two li { padding: .34rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
            font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .two li:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .two li span { display: block; color: var(--muted); font-size: .78rem; }

  /* 못 보는 것을 눈에 띄게 둔다 — 아래로 밀면 안 읽는다 */
  .warn {
    font-size: .86rem; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding: .8rem .9rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--card); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }

  .dist { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .dist li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr 6rem; align-items: center;
             gap: .6rem; padding: .3rem 0; }
  @media (max-width: 480px) { .dist li { grid-template-columns: 6.6rem 1fr 5rem; gap: .4rem; } }
  .k { font-size: .8rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .bar { height: 1.05rem; border-radius: 3px; background: var(--accent); width: var(--w);
         min-width: 2px; }
  .sa { opacity: .5; } .sb { opacity: 1; } .sc { opacity: .5; } .sd { opacity: .28; }
  .v { font-size: .8rem; color: var(--muted); text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .v b { color: var(--ink); font-size: .92rem; }

  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .85rem; }
  th, td { padding: .45rem .55rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); text-align: right;
           white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  th { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .03em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
       font-weight: 700; }
  th:first-child, td.nm { text-align: left; white-space: normal; }
  td.tot { font-weight: 700; }
  .pc { color: var(--muted); font-size: .76rem; }
  /* 폰에서 다섯 칸이 화면 밖으로 나가지 않게 — 3번이 8/8 에 그 자리에서 걸렸다 */
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    table { font-size: .78rem; }
    th, td { padding: .38rem .28rem; }
    .pc { display: block; }
  }

  .src ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; font-size: .85rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7; }
  .src li { margin: .45rem 0; }
  .src b { color: var(--ink); }
  .back { margin-top: 2.5rem; font-size: .9rem; }
</style>
